     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br>On Friday, April 18, 2014 8:33:03 AM UTC-5, Sam Dickson wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I have longitudinal data but not every subject is measured at every time point.  I want to use an unstructured covariance matrix in a multivariate normal with the ultimate goal of comparing variance between timepoints.</div><div> </div><div>I have the following model:</div><div> </div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><font color=\"#666600\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">data </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">{</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">int</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">lower </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">int</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">lower </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> k</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">k</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> x</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">];</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">int</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">lower </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> upper </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> group3</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">];</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">int</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">lower </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> upper </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> group4</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">];</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  real</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">lower </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> sigmax</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  real smean</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  real eta</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">}</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">{</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">lower </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&gt;[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">k</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma_scale</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">k</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> mu</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  corr_matrix</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">k</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma_corr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  real G3</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  real G4</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">}</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>model </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">{</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">k</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> k</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  mu </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">~</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">smean</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> sigmax</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">);</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma_scale</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">~</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> uniform</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0.0001</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> sigmax</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">);</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma_corr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">~</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> lkj_corr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">eta</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">);</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> diag_matrix</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma_scale</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> cholesky_decompose</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma_corr</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">);</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">for</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">i </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">in</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>    x</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">i</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">~</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> multi_normal_cholesky</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">mu </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">+</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> G3</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">group3</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">i</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">+</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> G4</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">group4</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">i</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">],</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">);</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">}</span></font></div></code></div><br>There are a total of 18 subjects at three time points I care about, but I can exclude 4 subjects to have no missing data.  When I do, the above model runs great.</div><div> </div><div>I can assume that the data are missing at random.  I have seen there are ways to fit missing data with a prior, but I&#39;m having trouble adapting that to work in this multivariate setting.  The key is that I don&#39;t know how to read in the 18 x 3 matrix in such a way that I can separate the missing and non-missing data without using NAs.</div><div> </div><div>There appears to be a hint in <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/stan-users/5KBr-xlunQM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!topic/stan-users/5KBr-xlunQM&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792466687253000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0P0A5b89msA9V4oi6vb7GW\">this post</a>, but the ultimate resolution wasn&#39;t clear enough for me to be able to implement it.  Can anyone help me to understand how to implement stan&#39;s missing data paradigm with multivariate data?</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
