  {
   "id": "XmpNOwIVsEcJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2014-04-18T14:54:29Z",
   "subject": "Re: Missing longitudinal data",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, April 18, 2014 9:33:03 AM UTC-4, Sam Dickson wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I have longitudinal data but not every subject is measured at every time point.  I want to use an unstructured covariance matrix in a multivariate normal with the ultimate goal of comparing variance between timepoints.</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>OK, although it would usually be better to use a structured covariance matrix for longitudinal data.  <br><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I can assume that the data are missing at random.  I have seen there are ways to fit missing data with a prior, but I&#39;m having trouble adapting that to work in this multivariate setting.  The key is that I don&#39;t know how to read in the 18 x 3 matrix in such a way that I can separate the missing and non-missing data without using NAs.</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>You need to also pass in an 18 x 3 binary array that is coded as 1 if the corresponding observation is observed and 0 if it is missing. I call this array R, BDA calls it I (although it is not an identity matrix), and some people reverse code it and call it M.<br> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div> There appears to be a hint in <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/stan-users/5KBr-xlunQM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!topic/stan-users/5KBr-xlunQM&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792466687255000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0O1TAZSmstUAMVzaPzXwto\">this post</a>, but the ultimate resolution wasn&#39;t clear enough for me to be able to implement it.  Can anyone help me to understand how to implement stan&#39;s missing data paradigm with multivariate data?</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>The paradigm is basically &quot;don&#39;t have missing data&quot;. Failing that, you could modify the attachments to your case. The main points are<br><ul><li>Pass in the R array so you know what is observed and what is missing (you also have to recode the missing values to be something like -999 for Stan to accept them but the -999 never actually gets used)</li><li>Declare the missing values as parameters to be estimated<br></li><li>In the model block, use the corresponding elements of R to make vectors that have a mix of observed data and proposed values for those that are missing originally<br></li><li>Call multi_normal_cholesky on those vectors (although it is better to use the onion method to create the Cholesky factor than to Cholesky factorize the covariance matrix)</li></ul><p>Ben</p><p><br></p></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
