     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Pass in the R array so you know what is observed and what is missing (you also have to recode the missing values to be something like -999 for Stan to accept them but the -999 never actually gets used)<blockquote style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\" class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><ul><li>Declare the missing values as parameters to be estimated<br></li><li>In the model block, use the corresponding elements of R to make vectors that have a mix of observed data and proposed values for those that are missing originally<br></li><li>Call multi_normal_cholesky on those vectors (although it is better to use the onion method to create the Cholesky factor than to Cholesky factorize the covariance matrix) </li></ul></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Is the onion method better because of speed issues or is there some stability issue I should be concerned about?</div><div></div></div></div></div></blockquote><div><br>Speed, RAM, numerical precision, geometry, etc., but none of that would be noticieable for a 3x3 matrix. I just wrote it for the general case of arbitrary K (although the more general case would also model R).<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div>"
